Patch 0085: the IOP emulator can run one ahead-of-time translated module natively.
What: ps2x::iop::registerNativeModule() registers one module (IopNativeModule in include/ps2x/iop/iop_native_module.h:
code range, entry pcs, FNV-1a hash of the code, run function, optional stale/resetStale). In runCpu, at an entry pc
with no branch or load pending, no queued guest callback, no interrupt deliverable and the next DMA completion
not yet due, the emulator calls run(cpu, memory, budget) with budget = min(slice left, cycles to the next DMA
completion) and adds the returned count to totalInstructions and totalCycles (one instruction == one cycle, as in the
interpreter). The code hash is checked once, before first use (a mismatch disables the module); from then on
IopMemory watches the code range (one extra compare in markOwned(), the single point of every RAM write) and any
write into it, by the guest, a loader or a DMA, or a store that run() handed to the interpreter (stale()), disables
the module for good. With no module registered the hook costs one bool test in the interpreter loops.
DON_IOP_NATIVE=0 turns the module off; DON_IOP_NATIVE_VERIFY=1 replays every native stretch from the same state in the
interpreter, compares CPU state and the whole RAM, keeps the interpreter's result and prints `[don-iop] native verify
... DIFFERENCE ...` (first 20) and a summary line every 65536 calls. Messages: `[don-iop] native module NAME
active|disabled: ...`.
Verified (before the fix below): ps2xIOP builds (Release, g++); all five ps2xIOP test programs pass, including the new
tests/iop_native_module_tests.cpp (hand-written module over a synthetic program: same result and instruction count as the
interpreter, hash guard, disable on write, DON_IOP_NATIVE=0, verify mode with a correct and a wrong module); and
tests/test_iop_emit.py::test_generated_module_through_the_emulator_matches_the_interpreter (the emitter's output for a
synthetic loop, registered and run through IopEmulator: interpreter, native and verify give the same start result and
instruction count, 0 verify differences, 8004 native instructions). Not run with the game yet.
Original to this project. Applies after 0082.
Fix of 2026-10-19 (found with the state hash of tools/run/perf_fight.py, which first differed at blank 2520 by one IOP cycle):
(1) A native stretch is clamped to the next DMA completion, so after it totalCycles == nextDmaCycle. The interpreter
services a due completion at the end of its tight loop, before the next instruction; runCpu's native path at the loop
head did `continue` and ran one more instruction through step() first, so the completion (and the handler it runs)
came one cycle late. Now the head path services DMA completions and guest callbacks right after a native stretch.
(2) Verify mode was itself corrupting the run: restoreRamRaw() cleared the whole pre-decoded table, and the tight loop
holds a pointer to the entry it decoded before calling tryNative(); when the stretch ran 0 instructions the loop
executed the cleared entry (reserved instruction, jump to 0x80000080, the thread ran a nop slide: ~5x the
instructions). It now only marks the entries invalid. Verify also did not model the interpreter's bookkeeping: it now
replays each stretch with the tight loop's own conditions and counting (executeDecoded, schedulePendingDma, one
instruction and one cycle each; the replay is the only thing that advances totalInstructions/totalCycles) and compares
CPU, RAM, instruction and cycle deltas and the event state (next DMA completion unchanged, no DMA started, no callback,
no deliverable interrupt, no import stub or sentinel inside the stretch).
Verified: a71d52ac... state hash with the module on and with DON_IOP_NATIVE=0 (perf_fight.py), see the journal.

